        ":question/problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}+\\frac{3}{2}?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/a6/a67681a546ecd949fd8cc3be1d5d0647a3cbf88c8809bdc2c620f0d42cb89c8b.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}+\\frac{3}{2},$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}$ and shift it $\\frac{3}{2}$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{5}{2}),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n![](images/7d/7dbfba1ee7e66fbc15858f668aacc27db4bdeebb3a7e28f243f2dc62216dda57.png)"
